  "topicCode": "1.1",
  "topicName": "EQ1: Why are some locations more at risk from tectonic hazards?",
  "text": "(i) Complete Figure 1 by calculating ∑d2.\n(ii) The formula for Spearman’s rank correlation coefficient value rs is given below;\nin this data set n is equal to 12.\n6∑d2\nrs = 1 −  n3 − n\nCalculate the value of rs to two decimal places for the data given.\nYou must show your working.\nrs = ..............................................................\n(iii) The tables below show the two hypotheses that are being tested and the\ncritical values of Spearman’s rank rs value when n = 12.\nNull hypothesis: There is no significant relationship between the\n% of silica and the % of volatile gases in these lava samples.\nAlternative hypothesis: There is a significant relationship between the\n% of silica and the % of volatile gases in these lava samples.\nConfidence level 0.10 0.05 0.01\nCritical value (90% significance) (95% significance) (99% significance)\n0.50 0.59 0.78\nUsing the Spearman’s rank correlation rs value calculated in (a)(ii), state which\nhypothesis can be accepted.",
  "ms": "Indicative content Mark\nAO3 (4 marks) (1)\n(1)\n(i) Award 1 mark for the sum of d2 column (Σ) = 30 Award 1 mark for the correct working of equation:\n1-6 X 30 123 -12 Award 1 mark for answers that round to R = 0.90 to 2 d.p (1)\nAllow errors carrying forward\n(ii) (1)\nAward 1 mark for rejection of null hypothesis as t value is more than critical value at all confidence levels and the acceptance of the alternative hypothesis that there is a significant relationship between the % of silica and the % of volatile gas in these lava samples.",
