  "eq": null,
  "eqConfident": false,
  "topicCode": null,
  "topicName": null,
  "text": "Study Figure 3 in the Resource Booklet.\n(i) Suggest one reason why the distribution of international migrants varies\nacross the UK.\n(ii) Explain why there are tensions between long-term residents and international\nmigrants in some areas but not others.",
  "ms": "Suggest one reason why the distribution of international migrants Mark number varies across the UK. (3)\nAO1 (2 marks)/AO2 (1 mark)\nAward 1 mark for analysing the resource to identify one possible reason the distribution of in-migrants varies across the UK and a further 2 marks of explanation up to a maximum 3 marks.\nFor example:\n Asylum seekers often arrive in south-east of country or at airports(1) so government relocates people to north or west of country(1) to reduce over-crowding and allow support services chance of providing enough help(1).\n International migrants choose to move to places with existing enclaves(1)where family provide support/housing (1) making life easier and safer for new arrivals(1).\n International migrants have been pre-recruited for agricultural work(1) so live in areas with unfilled agricultural jobs(1) e.g.\nraspberry picking in Herefordshire(1).\nAllow one mark for a detailed example.\nAccept other appropriate responses.\nExplain why there are tensions between long-term residents and international number migrants in some areas but not others.\nAO1 (3 marks)/AO2 (3 marks)\nFigure 3 should be used in answering the question, which might include direct reference to data or more general ‘connections/relationships’ triggered by Figure 3. Do not expect direct reference to the map, even for Level 3.\nAO1  Significant migration of people into/within UK has created uneven cultural patterns, for instance some international migrants live in rural areas.\n Frequent tensions over diversity of living spaces between long-term residents and recent in-migrants.\nAO2  High numbers of in-migrants in cities and towns (London, Manchester, Birmingham, Cardiff, Boston) as migrants find support and safety there, but this may raise tensions as balance of numbers changes.\n Remote areas such as northern Scotland have few in-migrants so tension levels may be reduced, as communities experience little change.\n But some remote have been directed to take migrants who are forced to relocate there. This may cause high levels of tension 18  Perception of tension may not be the same for in-migrants and long-term residents. In some communities ‘white flight’ has occurred.\n Wealthy migrants, e.g. from Russia bring cash to invest in housing leading to a rise in house prices/rent which is often resented by local people.\n Some communities celebrate assimilation of cultures and schools and community groups help build understanding (e.g. Aik Saath).\n Poverty and job shortages may build resentment between groups in areas experiencing deprivation.\n Discrimination and racist attitudes have persisted in places and build tensions and sometimes aggression (e.g. Tottenham, Burnley, Luton).\nLevel Mark Descriptor\nLevel 1 0 1–2 No rewardable material.\nLevel 2\nLevel 3 3–4  Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant.\n5–6 (AO1)\n Understanding addresses a narrow range of geographical ideas, which lack detail. (AO1)\n Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding, which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies. (AO1)\n Understanding addresses a range of geographical ideas, which are not fully detailed and/or developed. (AO1)\n Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. (AO1)\n Understanding addresses a broad range of geographical ideas, which are detailed and fully developed. (AO1)",
  "pageFrom": 15,
  "pageTo": 16,
  "pdf": "Exam questions PDFs/A-Level Geography/Paper 2 (Human)/Questions/June 2019 QP.pdf",
  "rb": "Exam questions PDFs/A-Level Geography/Paper 2 (Human)/Resource Booklet/June 2019 RB.pdf",
