  "inSpec": false,
  "eq": null,
  "eqConfident": false,
  "topicCode": null,
  "topicName": null,
  "text": "Study Figure 5 below.\nKey Education level % of total\n\nLower primary 25\n(age 6-8) 19\n\nUpper primary 100\n(age 9-11)\nLower secondary\n(age 12-14)\nUpper secondary\n(age 15-17)\nTOTAL\nFigure 5\nChildren enrolled in school in Madagascar, 2014\n(i) Draw a divided bar chart, using the key and the data in Figure 5.\n(ii) The total number of children aged 6-17 in Madagascar was 8.9 million. The\nnumber of children who were enrolled in school was 5.8 million.\nCalculate the percentage of children who were enrolled in school to one\ndecimal place.\nYou must show your working.\n.............................................................. (%)\n(iii) Suggest reasons why the number of years spent in education by Madagascar’s\nchildren could affect its economic development.",
  "ms": "Answer PMT\nAO3 (2 marks) Mark (1,1)\nNote key is in QP so there is no requirement to reproduce this.\nCorrectly drawn scale (1) and correctly drawn divided bar chart(1).\nThis could also be drawn horizontally.\nAlso accept correctly drawn scale (1) for a bar chart or histogram, but no mark for the wrong type of graph, as shown below. This scores (1).\n23 Answer Mark number (1,1)\nAO3 (2 marks)\n(1) for working and (1) for correct answer (to one decimal place)\n5.8 ÷ 8.9 x100 (1)\n= 65.2% (1)\nNo working, correct answer (1)\nAccept other working that leads to a correct answer.(1,1)\nCorrect working but wrong numbers (1)\nAnswer must be correct to one decimal place.\nAccept answers that are not written on the lines.\n24 Suggest reasons why the number of years spent in education by Madagascar’s number children could affect its economic development.\nAO1 (3 marks)/AO2 (3 marks)\nNote the candidate’s bar chart provides a resource to be used in answering this questions.\nAO1  Education is central to economic development as it involves investment in human capital, both at individual and national level.\n Education also helps understanding and assertion of human rights.\n Access to education and standards of achievement vary among countries.\nAO2  Lower primary is where people learn to read and write; upper secondary is where they will learn skills needed for tertiary employment.\n Low levels of upper secondary education are likely to lead to few young people being able to access higher education,  Therefore the earning capacity of the person and the skill levels of Madagascar will be are held back e.g. few doctors, engineers, IT consultants.\n GDP capacity is restricted if literacy, numeracy and IT skills of its people are limited as majority only have primary level education. Many are likely to be subsistence farmers like their parents.\n Gender inequality is likely to be more marked if few access education beyond mid-teens. Girls likely to start child-bearing at young age.\n Capacity for participation in democracy and decision-making is increased with education. Empowerment is encouraged.\n Country is likely to stay dependent on primary products like coffee/cotton.\n Tax revenue remains low, so country cannot afford competitive wages for trained staff e.g. nurses so they leave for higher earnings in South Africa  Failure to attract FDI/TNCs due to few years in education for many.\nLevel Mark Descriptor\nLevel 1 0 1–2 No rewardable material.\nLevel 2 3–4  Demonstrates isolated or generic elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. (AO1)\n Applies knowledge and understanding to geographical information inconsistently. Connections/relationships between stimulus material and the question may be irrelevant. (AO2)\n Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding, which 25 Level Mark Descriptor\nLevel 3 5–6 is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies. (AO1)\n Applies knowledge and understanding to geographical information to find some relevant connections/relationships between stimulus material and the question. (AO2)\n Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. (AO1)\n Applies knowledge and understanding to geographical information logically to find fully relevant connections/relationships between stimulus material and the question. (AO2)\n26",
  "pageFrom": 22,
  "pageTo": 24,
