  "text": "Assess the strengths and weaknesses of emerging powers in challenging for\nsuperpower status.\n\n\nShaping Places",
  "ms": "Assess the strengths and weaknesses of emerging powers in challenging for number superpower status.\nAO1 (3 marks)/AO2 (9 marks)\nResponses that demonstrate only AO1 without any AO2 should be awarded marks as follows:\n• Level 1 AO1 performance: 1 mark\n• Level 2 AO1 performance: 2 marks\n• Level 3 AO1 performance: 3 marks.\nAO1\n• A superpower is a country with the ability to project its dominating power and influence anywhere in the world.\n• Emerging superpowers are countries with a large role in one of more superpower characteristics, and with growing influence. E.g. China, Brazil, India and Russia\n• There are six superpower characteristics; ecomomic i.e. GDP, Political i.e.\nvoting power in IGOs, miltiary i.e. blue water and nuclear, cultural i.e.\nmedia and TNCs, demographc i.e. population size and resources i.e.\nenergy\n• Challenges facing superpowers are tensions over acquistion of physical resources, issues surrounding counterfeiting, overlapping spheres of influence, cultural, political and environmental tensions\nAO2 Country Strengths Weaknesses Brazil • Abundant natural • Lacking economic resources e.g. iron ore, infrastructure i.e. poor timber, soya, crude oil investment in road and rail\n• Produces half of South • Very high lending interest America’s GDP so could rate around 16.25% be classes as a regional • Spends 60% of South superpower America’s miltiary budget,\n• It is self sufficient in food however their military is and energy meaning it is the least signficant of all of energy secure and less the BRICs.\neffected by ongoing global • They are reliant of primary inflation.\nproducts for export rather\n• Set to be the world’s fifth- than manufacturing rather larges economy by 2050 than adding value to boost overtaking Japan, their economy Assess the strengths and weaknesses of emerging powers in challenging for number superpower status.\nAnswer Germany and Russia. • Politically less stable with Russia accusations of corruption which could deter FDI India\n• Wealth of natural • Russia’s population has China resources e.g. oil, natural decreased between 1991- gas, coal 2015 and has seen high losses due to the recent\n• Skilled workforce conflict.\n• World largest country by • The economy is unbalanced landmass with the elite controlling a large prportion of Russia’s wealth.\n• Much of Russia’s aircraft and naval stock is ageing reducing their hard power\n• High levels of economic sanctions due to Ukrainian invasion\n• Current political isolation by the Western world\n• World’s largest democracy • Large public debt as a with 672 million voters percentage of GDP (+74%)\n• Bollywood is the world’s which has led to the largest film industry, reduxtion in the value of the producing around 1200 rupee films a year • India has had high level of\n• Second largest population environmentla problems in the world meaning they with severe pollution have a large working • The world’s largest emitter population to help boost of carbon dioxide the economy. • 20% of Indians live in\n• Strong IT and service severe poverty, therefore it sector struggles to attract migrants\n• Founding member of the • Bollywood does not have the UN same global reach as Hollywood reducing their\n• Some natural resources soft power.\ne.g. coal and natural gas\n• The world’s largest army • Environmental issues and the have clear becoming an obstacle to strengths in submarines sustainable growth and combat aircafts • Increasing income resulting in strong hard inequality resulting in social power.\ntension\n• Communist one-party • The population is ageing state with a very strong due to the previous one manufacturing base child policy. By 2040, a allowing it to become the quarter of Chinese will be ‘workshop of the world’. over 60.\nAssess the strengths and weaknesses of emerging powers in challenging for number superpower status.\n• The largest population in • The open door policy to FDI the world therefore is has has resulted in a heavy the benefits of being a reliance on foreign markets ‘demographic’ superpower making it vulnerable to\n• Industrially competitive external global factors.\n• Strong foreign direct • The Covid pandemic highlighted cracks in investment China’s healthcare system\n• Large deposits of coal, iron and combined with an ore, petroleum and natural gas ageing population this could cause social problems in the future.\nAssessment:\n• Best responses will consider how likely the chosen superpowers will be in challenging for superpower status and whether they are likely to dominant the current superpower of the US.\n• Candidates may consider that Brazil can be considered a regional superpowers due to the fact that they dominate their respective continents in terms of economic power, but currently don’t dominate globally.\n• Candidates may consider the challenges facing emerging superpowers in challenging for superpower status and consider which challenge is more significant.\nLevel Mark Descriptor\nLevel 1 0 1–4 No rewardable material.\nLevel 2 5–8 • Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant.\n(AO1)\n• Applies knowledge and understanding of geographical information/ideas, making limited logical connections/relationships. (AO2)\n• Applies knowledge and understanding of geographical information/ideas to produce an interpretation with limited relevance and/or support. (AO2)\n• Applies knowledge and understanding of geographical information/ideas to make unsupported or generic judgements about the significance of few factors, leading to an argument is unbalanced or lacks coherence. (AO2)\n• Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding, which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies.\n(AO1)\n• Applies knowledge and understanding of geographical information/ideas logically, making some relevant Level Mark Descriptor\nLevel 3 9–12 connections/relationships. (AO2)\n• Applies knowledge and understanding of geographical information/ideas to produce a partial but coherent interpretation that is mostly relevant and supported by evidence. (AO2)\n• Applies knowledge and understanding of geographical information/ideas to make judgements about the significance of some factors, to produce an argument that may be unbalanced or partially coherent. (AO2)\n• Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. (AO1)\n• Applies knowledge and understanding of geographical information/ideas logically, making relevant connections/relationships. (AO2)\n• Applies knowledge and understanding of geographical information/ideas to produce a full and coherent interpretation that is relevant and supported by evidence. (AO2)\n• Applies knowledge and understanding of geographical information/ideas to make supported judgements about the significance of factors throughout the response, leading to a balanced and coherent argument. (AO2)",
